\f3\fs26 Agent Jones marched up and down trying to work out why the new Director had summoned him to his office. He had fucked up a few times in the last two days and he knew he was a prime candidate for contract termination. The first order of business for most new directors was to clear out the deadwood and he was feeling dry and combustible. He walked the last few metres to Damien\'92s Office as a condemned prisoner, his wrists unconsciously drawn together by invisible bindings, head low and back hunched. \
\'93Agent Jones, I have been reviewing your performance of late and I think we have a few issues. Your blank disappeared on a simple termination and you lost a Gyges Disc. Have you any idea what you have done? In an effort to cover your pathetic backside you have left a trail of destruction and misery. I cannot see any purpose for the extreme prejudice displayed in killing the entire Kalheri family. It has blown up in to a major police investigation! I am struggling to find a reason not to terminate your contract here and now.\'94 He opened his draw and removed a particle pistol, placing it gently on the desk in front of him.\
\'93Sir, I have been through all this with your predecessor. The techs took the blank to the wrong morgue without notifying me. He was located and transferred to our labs. In any case, we got nothing from our examination. There is just one more termination to effect and the matter will be finalised.\'94 Agent Jones\'92 eyes were affixed on the tiny red lights of the gun, flashing in regular pattern. It was charged and the safety was off. His worst case scenario unfolding.\
\'93You want to kill more people?\'94 He picked up the pistol and casually pointed it at the frightened agent.\
\'93Just one, sir, but I have been unable to identify him. I think he is a cleaner and he\'92s got the disc, but that\'92s as far as the trail has lead so far.\'94 He failed to tell the Director that he had stood no more than one metre away from that very cleaner during a 116 floor elevator trip and failed to notice anything, apart from the annoying trolley. He was already in enough shit and he knew from painful experience that revealing too much of the truth will fuck you up every time.\
\'93Who else was on this with you?\'94 Damien was insistent\
\'93Apart from the techs, no-one.\'94\
\'93What did they see?\'94 \
\'93A cleaner is all I got from them.\'94\
\'93Did they record anything?\'94 For the purposes of finding out who fucked up during covert operations, it was standard procedure to record everything possible on audio and video.\
\'93No point. There was a gyges disc in play.\'94 Agent Jones, just managing to stifle a smug grin, was very grateful there was no possibility of any electronic record of his fuck-up surfacing. It was bad enough to make mistakes, but far worse for those errors to be captured for posterity. Enduring the ridicule of his murderous contemporaries was more than Agent Jones\'92 ego could tolerate. \
\'93You need to re-interview the techs. Use advanced interview protocols if you think it necessary.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94 He could not wait to torture those pair of clowns.\
\'93After that I have a very special job for you. This will be your last chance, your very last chance to impress me, Agent Jones. We will be digging up the old Bio-Tech research facility of Jameson Hardy. It is a level 10 operation and you will head the security team.\'94 The Director did not tell him the likelihood of death was very high. \'93Shakti and his team are still here, taking statements and poking around so proceed with discretion.\'94\
\'93Thank you, sir.\'94 He did not betray himself, but digging up weapons grade bio-matter did not seem like a good idea and the more he thought about it the worse the idea became. He did not know what horrors lay buried beneath 10 metres of concrete and was not all that keen to find out.
